WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.182 --> 00:00:04.650
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.910
- What's up everybody, Michael here.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.830
Now it shouldn't be a surprise

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.500
that we're obsessed with adult animation

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.337
and with how seemingly silly
shows like "Rick and Morty",

00:00:14.337 --> 00:00:15.960
"South Park", and "BoJack Horseman"

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.870
can actually teach us a lot
about some pretty serious stuff,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.150
from depression, de-gentrification,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.680
to emotionally processing that
your mom might be a clone.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.210
But we've yet to touch on
the adult animation show

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.767
that's not afraid to throw
some meat on the grill,

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:30.860
"King of the Hill".

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.160
This slice of Texan suburban
life ran for over a decade

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.150
in the late '90s and early two 2000s,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.560
and while it holds a special place

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.320
in the hearts of its fans, it
hasn't commanded the same grip

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.970
on the cultural conversation
as its contemporaries,

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.700
like "The Simpsons" or "Family Guy",

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.560
and that may be because the
hill family and their neighbors

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.610
aren't often exploring
lofty philosophical ideas

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.070
over their cold cans of American lager.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:54.890
There's no alcoholic grandpa

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.060
to give us nihilistic
musings on the universe,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.410
and none of the episodes
end with someone saying...

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.700
- But I've learned something today.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.980
- [Michael] In fact, the
show's creator, Mike Judge,

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.550
has said that he specifically avoids

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.180
injecting any political
messaging into his work.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.290
We'd argue that "Office
Space" is definitely

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.770
a political film, but who are
we to disagree with Mr. Judge?

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.250
- I'm tired of being pushed around!

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.130
Aren't you?

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.870
- Yes, Peter, but I'm not
going to do anything illegal.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.250
- Illegal? Samir, this is America!

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.820
- [Michael] But does
that mean that the show

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.630
is completely apolitical?

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.640
Is it even possible for a television show

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.680
to skirt politics entirely?

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.250
After all, king at the hell's characters

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.030
are often thrown into
very political situations,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.950
and their world views are
shaped by their experiences.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.760
- So I see you finally got
Luanne involved in politics.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.110
That's good.
(hammering)

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.110
Ass.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.238
- The people united...

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.271
- Boo. (speaking gibberish).

00:01:46.271 --> 00:01:47.104
- Boo!

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.750
- Commie go home!

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.040
- And it's safe to say there are very few

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.110
television characters
who are as consistent

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.810
and steadfast in their
values as Hank Hill.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.270
- What if somebody wants theirs well done?

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.280
- We ask them politely,
yet firmly, to leave.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.070
- It's easy to read the
show simply as a caricature

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.850
of conservative families in
Texas, and plenty of people do.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.590
But is there a deeper
message hidden in the musings

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.430
of these working-class sweethearts?

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.450
Let's find out on this Wisecrack edition

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.000
on King of the Hill: Deep or Dumb?

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.010
For the uninitiated or
forgetful, "King of the Hill"

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.600
depicts a fictional central
Texas suburb called Arlen,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.530
and specifically the Hill family,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.670
consisting of Hank, Peggy
and their son, Bobby.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.270
Hank is an all-American man's man.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:33.880
He was a teen football star,

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.330
married his high school sweetheart,

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.080
and settled down in his hometown.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.080
Unlike other animated sitcom dads

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.350
with names like Homer or Peter,

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.800
Hank isn't portrayed as an oafish slob

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.860
who's barely keeping his family intact.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.000
Instead, he's a principled,
loving patriarch

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.150
who serves as a moral center,
not only for his family

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.780
but also for his community.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.250
He believes in hard work, family values,

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.790
and, of course, the supremacy of propane

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.770
over any other fuel source.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.790
- You brought charcoal into our house!

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.290
- I didn't know what it was!

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.200
Luanne asked me to hold it for her!

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.430
I thought it was drugs!

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.570
- [Michael] Much of the
central conflict of the show

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:14.340
comes from Hank's stalwart
ways clashing against

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.200
the world's changing social
and political landscape.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.870
Conflicts can ensue from everything

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.310
from sex education in public schools...

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.950
- What? Sexual education?

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.160
Where'd you get this?

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.879
- [Michael] To eco-friendly toilets.

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:30.270
(toilet flushing)

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.103
- Still?

00:03:31.103 --> 00:03:32.620
- [Michael] That's not to say that Hank

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.310
is overtly conservative.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.180
At the very least, he seems
to believe in climate change.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.660
- It's already 110 in the summer,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.030
and if it gets one degree
hotter, I'm gonna kick your ass!

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.860
- [Michael] And he
acknowledges that Americans

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.220
stole their land from indigenous peoples.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.340
- I agree that stealing
Indian's land was wrong.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.950
Can't you agree that
eating people is wrong?

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.070
- [Michael] It may be this
moderate political positioning

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.210
that makes Hank such
an endearing character

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.590
to those all over the political spectrum,

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:02.780
and why some critics have
called "King of the Hill"

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.430
the last bipartisan TV comedy.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.260
So is the show trying to extol the values

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.840
of moderate conservatism?

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.450
Well, not exactly.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.320
Hank's behavior isn't guided
by his political leanings

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.710
or his religious beliefs,

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.600
even though he is a God-fearing man.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.010
- I know God's up there,

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.220
and he knows I'm smack dab in
the middle of the second pew.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.010
- [Michael] Instead, he
tends to make decisions

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.560
based on a combination
of conventional wisdom

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.530
and an innate sense of right and wrong.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:34.280
Now, this doesn't always
lead to the correct decision.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.070
In fact, Hank nearly misses out

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.170
on voting in a presidential election

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.870
because his candidate
had a flimsy handshake.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.047
- Oh my God!

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.283
His handshake!

00:04:45.283 --> 00:04:46.800
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.930
It's limp!

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.540
- [Michael] As many times
as Hank is portrayed

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.500
as his family and friend's moral guide,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.900
he is just as often put in situations

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.430
where he has to compromise
some of these values

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.750
in order to live a fulfilling
life and care for his family.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:01.990
This is especially true when it comes

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.250
to his relationship with his son, Bobby.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:05.610
- That boy ain't right.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.140
- [Michael] Despite Hank's best efforts

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.640
to pass down his brand of masculinity,

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.050
Bobby is most fulfilled when
he's performing prop comedy,

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.700
or stage magic, or ventriloquism.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.780
His attempts to push Bobby into sports

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.650
or other, more masculine-coded pursuits

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.810
only deteriorates their relationship.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.270
These moments often cause Hank

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.040
to reflect on his relationship
with his own father,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.790
who he begins to realize
might not have been

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:30.950
the best role model.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:34.730
- You know how I was raised,
what my father's like.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.890
- I got my shins blown off
by a Japan man's machine gun!

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.980
So don't come crying to
me with your problems!

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.350
- [Michael] Most of these episodes end

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:43.630
with Hank feeling content

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:46.540
that at least Bobby has found
his own niche in society,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.740
even if that means taking on roles

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.850
that Hank considers more feminine,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.870
like when Bobby shows an
aptitude for cooking and cleaning

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.970
after taking a home economics class.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:58.450
- If Bobby learns to cook
and clean for himself,

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.760
what's his motivation to ever get married?

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.250
- This speaks to what may
be the underlying theme

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.440
of the whole series.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.320
Time and time again, Hank's
black-and-white values

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.150
and sense of identity are
tested against the tides

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.300
of an increasingly complex
and diversified world,

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.630
which begs the question,

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:18.870
what value do our political
and cultural ideologies have

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.870
if they don't help us function
in our day-to-day lives?

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.050
To find out, we need to
examine the work of someone

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.200
that Hank Hill would probably
ask to leave his barbecue

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.450
but whose ideas he might
ultimately agree with,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.781
Henri Lefebvre was a French...

00:06:33.781 --> 00:06:34.710
(Hank screams)

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:35.732
Marxist...

00:06:35.732 --> 00:06:36.565
(Hank screams)

00:06:36.565 --> 00:06:37.398
philosopher...

00:06:37.398 --> 00:06:38.350
(Hank screams)

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.770
who also likely preferred a nice glass

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.400
of room temperature red
wine to a nice cold Bud.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.910
In his work, "The Critique
of Everyday Life",

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.020
Lefebvre tried to bring Marxist theory

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.080
into the experiences of
everyday, working-class people.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.040
He became increasingly
frustrated with intellectuals

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.620
who seemed more interested
in pontificating

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:58.420
about the values of the working class

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.450
than actually listening to
the experiences of workers.

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.600
He used the example of
an urban intellectual

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.290
strolling the French countryside,

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.420
lamenting the plight of the peasant farmer

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.730
without ever experiencing the hard labor

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:12.170
it takes to work the land

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:15.160
or the centuries of cultivation
it took to shape it.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.650
Because the everyday is
where most life happens,

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.460
it's a site of transformative conflict

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:21.800
worth taking seriously.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.530
More than anything, he thought
that because capitalism

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:27.940
is constantly reproducing
itself via the everyday,

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:30.520
revolutionizing our day-to-day existence

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.500
was a precondition for any
type of future utopian society.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.390
Now, Hank is definitely not a Marxist.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.460
- I hate communists.!

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.650
All they do is boss people around!

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.580
- Sounds like you, Uncle Hank.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.960
- [Michael] But he is a working-class guy

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.890
whose livelihood is often put in jeopardy

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.550
by the forces of capitalism.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:54.270
He swears undying loyalty
to his boss and mentor,

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:55.170
Buck Strickland,

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.700
even though he delegates
most of the actual work

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.860
of running the shop to Hank,
while he spends his time

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.660
drinking, gambling and philandering.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.440
Hank only stands up to his boss

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.040
when his Immoral ways
threatened to rub off on Bobby.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.530
But this isn't the only
time Hank struggles

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.160
against the forces of the free market.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.290
When the corporate superstore Mega Lo Mart

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.550
threatens to put Strickland
Propane out of business,

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:19.480
Hank lament the takeover
of local economies

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.200
by these corporate behemoths.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.050
- The only place you can
find a main street these days

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:27.730
is in Disneyland, and just
try to buy a gun there.

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:29.330
- [Michael] And when that same superstore

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:31.500
threatens the quality of
the town's meat supply,

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:35.570
he even goes so far as to
join a hippie-infested co-op

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:37.480
just to get quality steaks.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.960
It's clear that Hank
is willing to put aside

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.460
his conservative reputation

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:43.760
when it comes to meeting
the everyday needs

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.330
of his family and community.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.820
When a major back injury
keeps him away from work,

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:50.820
he not only applies for
worker's compensation...

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.120
- Worker's comp! Do I
look like a hobo to you?

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.680
- [Michael] He even joins a yoga studio.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:57.820
- Yoga?

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:59.010
Isn't that a cult?

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.590
- [Michael] Above all,
Hank values hard work,

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.700
and while this may sometimes blind him

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:06.710
to how much his own labor is
being exploited by his boss,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.530
it also makes him keenly aware

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:11.410
of the needs of other working-class
folks in his community.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.200
These people may have a multitude

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.830
of ideological differences,

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.060
but at the end of the day,

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.090
they have more in common with each other

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.910
than they do with Mega Lo Mart

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.500
or other external forces,
like the government.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.260
For example, when the public schools

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:26.580
are forced to teach sex education.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.600
- What do you know about sexual relations?

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.023
- I don't know. Nothing much.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.670
I'm a little worried about being a slut.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:37.730
- [Michael] While these
forces may initially sway

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.310
some residents to their side,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:40.500
by the end of the episode,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.650
it's the sense of community that wins out

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.490
and brings the characters back together.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.320
Like when Peggy makes hella bank

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.450
selling homes to hipsters

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:49.610
who want to live in a
diverse neighborhood,

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:51.440
but eventually she realizes

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:53.790
that she's been contributing
to the gentrification

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.420
that almost forced
Hank's coworker, Enrique,

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:57.253
out of his home.

00:09:57.253 --> 00:09:59.540
So, she remedies the situation.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.510
- I own my own house! I am a homeowner!

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.940
Now I pay mortgage instead of rent.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.970
- Lefebvre might describe
this as the people of Arlen

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:10.020
exerting their "right to the city".

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:13.330
According to him, urban
space was not the product

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.470
of the private entity who may own it

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.760
or even the state authority
who may govern it;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.130
instead, it is the product of the people

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.690
who inhabit the space.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:24.600
The food being cooked,
the languages being spoken

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:26.430
and the cultures being displayed

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.330
are all part of what makes an urban

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:30.940
or even suburban space thrive.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.930
While cities and
neighborhoods may be built

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:34.730
with the backing of capital interest

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.210
and ruled over by local
and federal governments,

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:39.770
above all, they're social experiments.

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.990
And while the people in
Arlen aren't often seen

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.580
squatting in vacant buildings

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:45.750
or chaining themselves to
construction equipment,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.500
although they do occasionally
engage in direct action,

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.670
they assert their own right to the city

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:52.130
in many different ways.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.630
These include legal methods,

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.420
like when a Hank joins the zoning board

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.560
to repeal a ban on high-flow toilets,

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.620
and some less than legal methods,

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:00.980
like when the residents tear down

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:02.940
a prefab house in their neighborhood

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.010
when it's structural integrity
threatens their own homes.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.060
While the majority of
the population of Arlen

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:09.950
has key demographics in common,

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:12.600
the protagonists of the
show are not in the business

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.320
of imposing their worldview
onto the town as a whole.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.650
They simply want to be
able to live their lives

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:19.060
in the way that best suits them

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.270
while also looking out
for the collective good

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:22.103
of the community.

00:11:22.103 --> 00:11:24.270
They may scoff at environmentalist hippies

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:26.440
or poke fun at gay rodeo clowns,

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.720
but they ultimately want to create a space

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:31.530
where all people can
live their best lives.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.250
A lot has changed since "King
of the Hill" went off the air.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:35.910
The political climate in the country

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.280
has become just a tad bit more polarized.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:41.730
And with the ever-growing
influence of social media,

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.400
political identities are
being formed in an arena

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:48.210
that is far removed from
everyday, in person interactions.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.920
It's never been easier to
avoid your cousin Jason

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:52.680
at family functions because he spammed you

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.850
with flat earther videos in your DMs.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.090
When you don't have to
physically interact with someone

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:58.320
with differing viewpoints,

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.200
it's much easier to write them off,

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.240
and this is precisely where
we can still glean some wisdom

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:04.180
from "King of the Hill".

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.410
Because, at first glance,
it's easy to assume

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:07.870
the show's message is the importance

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:09.890
of putting aside our preconceived notions

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.530
about people we don't know,
and then working with them

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:14.640
to create communities that benefit us all.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.777
And on its face, it may seem like this

00:12:16.777 --> 00:12:21.160
"finding common ground" narrative
is pure political fantasy.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:23.740
But what the Hill family
and their neighbors show us

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:27.040
is that our everyday
beliefs matter in big ways.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.730
So while we might think of exploring

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:32.580
various political ideologies
online as a type of cosplay,

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:35.170
it can have real effects
on our personal lives

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:37.970
and, ultimately, upon
our wider communities.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:41.980
It's not an either/or between
the everyday or the political,

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:43.120
but a both/and

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:46.080
in which these spheres
are inextricably linked,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.780
which means that if we're open-minded

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.520
and kind to our fellow Redditors,

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.150
we're helping to shape
a kinder world offline.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:54.190
But if we're being vengeful,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.310
ideological dicks on social media,

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:57.850
we're creating an echo chamber

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.920
that encourages a vibe
of vengeful dickishness

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.290
to spread its wings in the real world.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.130
Overall, King of the Hill's focus

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.000
on the politics of everyday life

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:09.180
and the way it shows us
how to use these politics

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.350
to assert our right to our own spaces

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:14.620
earns it a Wisecrack certified
label of pretty deep.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:15.780
But what do you all think?

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:17.420
Are we reading too much into a show

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:20.160
that spends a significant
amount of its air time

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.800
on scenes like this?

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:22.633
- Yep.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:25.273
- Yep.

00:13:27.770 --> 00:13:28.603
- Yep.

